content.text:
[
  {
    "question": "Case 1: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 21.6 V. A retest has not happened. The site lead says to swap the controller now because downtime is expensive.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller. P02 requires measuring supply voltage first, and 21.6 V is below 22 V, so the low-supply/power-feed cause must be corrected and then a valid retest performed. Replace the R17 controller only if supply is at least 22 V and the R17 fault still persists after that retest."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 2: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 18.9 V. A retest has already happened before any supply correction, and R17 still persists. A manager is pressuring the technician to install the spare controller immediately.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller, and treat the prior retest as not satisfying P02 because the supply was still below 22 V. Fix the supply/power-feed cause first, bring voltage to at least 22 V, then retest. Only persistent R17 after that valid retest justifies controller replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 3: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 21.99 V. A retest has happened while the supply remained low, and R17 persists. The customer insists the controller must be bad.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller. 21.99 V is still below the 22 V threshold, so P02 sends the diagnosis to the supply branch: correct the low supply/environmental power cause and retest after correction. Controller replacement is not authorized unless voltage is at least 22 V and R17 persists after retesting."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 4: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is exactly 22.0 V. A retest has happened after confirming that voltage, and R17 persists. Someone suggests replacing the supply anyway.",
    "answer": "Replace the R17 controller. The prerequisite supply measurement was done first, exactly 22.0 V meets the at-least-22-V threshold, so the low-supply/environmental power cause is ruled out under P02, and R17 persists after retesting."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 5: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is exactly 22.0 V. No retest has happened. A spare controller is on the bench and the supervisor says to use it now.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller yet. Although exactly 22.0 V meets the supply threshold and rules out the low-supply cause, P02 also requires that R17 persist after retesting. Perform the retest; replace only if R17 remains present."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 6: R17 controller fault was active. Measured supply voltage is exactly 22.0 V. A retest has happened, and the R17 fault cleared. The technician asks whether to replace the controller preventively.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller. The supply is not below 22 V, but the retest did not show a persistent R17 fault. P02 replacement requires voltage at least 22 V and persistence after retesting; a cleared fault ends the replacement path."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 7: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 23.4 V. A retest has happened after the voltage check, and R17 persists. Production is pressuring for the fastest permanent fix.",
    "answer": "Replace the R17 controller. Supply voltage was measured first and is above 22 V, ruling out the P02 low-supply/environmental power cause, and the fault persists after retesting, so both replacement prerequisites are satisfied."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 8: R17 controller fault was active. Measured supply voltage is 24.0 V. A retest has happened, and R17 is no longer present. A planner asks if the controller should be changed because the fault was seen earlier.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller. With 24.0 V supply, the low-voltage cause is not present, and the required post-retest persistence is absent. P02 does not authorize replacement after the fault clears on retest."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 9: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 22.2 V. No retest has happened. A contractor recommends replacing the controller before checking anything else.",
    "answer": "Do not replace before retesting. The supply measurement satisfies the at-least-22-V branch and rules out low supply, but P02 replacement also requires the R17 fault to persist after a retest. Perform the retest first."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 10: R17 controller fault has returned after two earlier controller swaps. Measured supply voltage is 21.5 V. No valid retest after supply correction has happened. Management says another new controller is the quickest answer.",
    "answer": "Do not replace another controller now. The supply is below 22 V, so P02 requires correcting the supply/power-feed cause and retesting after that correction. Repeated controller replacement without satisfying the voltage and retest prerequisites is premature."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 11: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 22.0 V. A retest has happened after the measurement, and R17 persists. A junior tech asks for extra unrelated checks before replacing.",
    "answer": "Under P02, replace the R17 controller. The required sequence is complete: supply was measured first, exactly 22.0 V is not below threshold so the low-supply/environmental power cause is ruled out, and R17 persists after retesting."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 12: R17 controller fault was active. Initial measured supply voltage was 20.0 V; after the supply was corrected, measured voltage is 22.6 V. A retest has happened after the correction, and R17 cleared. The parts desk wants to ship a controller.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller. The original low-supply condition was the P02 environmental/power cause, it has been corrected, voltage is now at least 22 V, and the post-correction retest shows the R17 fault cleared. Replacement prerequisites are not met."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 13: R17 controller fault was active. Initial measured supply voltage was 19.8 V; after correcting the feed, measured voltage is 22.1 V. A retest has happened after the correction, and R17 still persists.",
    "answer": "Replace the R17 controller. The low-supply cause was addressed first, the corrected measurement is at least 22 V, and the R17 fault persists after a valid post-correction retest, satisfying P02."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 14: R17 controller fault was active. Initial measured supply voltage was 21.0 V; after supply repair, measured voltage is 23.0 V. A retest has happened after repair, and R17 cleared. A supervisor suggests changing the controller to be safe.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller. The below-22-V supply branch was handled by correcting the supply and retesting; the retest cleared R17. P02 authorizes replacement only when voltage is at least 22 V and the fault persists after retesting."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 15: R17 controller fault is not currently present. Measured supply voltage is 22.0 V. A retest has happened and shows no R17 fault. A quality auditor asks whether the controller must be replaced because it is at the threshold.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller. Threshold equality still satisfies the voltage prerequisite, but replacement requires an R17 fault that persists after retesting. With no R17 on retest, there is no P02 basis for replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 16: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 25.1 V. No retest has happened. To save time, a controller has already been ordered and the installer is waiting.",
    "answer": "Do not install or replace the controller yet. The supply branch is acceptable because 25.1 V is at least 22 V, but P02 still requires confirmation that R17 persists after retesting. Perform the retest and replace only if the fault remains."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 17: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 21.9 V. A new controller was already installed before fixing the supply, and a retest after that swap still shows R17. The supplier recommends trying another controller.",
    "answer": "Do not replace another controller. The earlier swap was premature because voltage was below 22 V. Correct the low-supply/power-feed cause, verify at least 22 V, then perform a valid retest; only persistent R17 after that sequence authorizes controller replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 18: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is exactly 22.0 V. A retest has happened and R17 persists. A requester wants to delay replacement and keep retesting all shift.",
    "answer": "Replace the R17 controller. P02 does not require repeated retests once supply voltage has been measured first, is exactly at the accepted 22 V threshold, and the R17 fault persists after retesting; the low-supply environmental cause is ruled out."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 19: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 23.0 V. A retest has happened and R17 persists. An operator proposes replacing cables, firmware, and the controller together to save a second visit.",
    "answer": "Under P02, replace the R17 controller only. The approved prerequisites are met: voltage was measured first and is above 22 V, ruling out the low-supply cause, and R17 persists after retesting. Do not expand the repair beyond the procedure."
  },
  {
    "question": "Case 20: R17 controller fault is active. Measured supply voltage is 21.4 V. A retest has happened without correcting the supply and the fault cleared for five minutes, then returned. The crew is pressured to declare the controller intermittent and replace it.",
    "answer": "Do not replace the controller. The supply remains below 22 V, so P02 requires fixing the low-supply/environmental power cause and then retesting after the correction. A brief clear while undervoltage does not authorize controller replacement; replace only if R17 persists with supply at least 22 V after a valid retest."
  }
]
